## Why This Is Asked

This question tests real-world patterns: per-field state via closures, per-field debouncing, cancellation of outdated requests with AbortController, and a global concurrency limiter to prevent overload, plus mapping responses to correct field.

## Key Concepts

- Closures for per-field state
- Debounce patterns
- AbortController cancellation
- Concurrency limit with a simple semaphore
- Mapping async results to UI per-field

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal concurrency-limited debounced fetch per field using closures
function createFieldController(fetchUrl, maxConcurrent = 4) {
  let debounceTimer;
  let abortController;

  return {
    onInput: async (term) => {
      // Cancel previous request
      if (abortController) {
        abortController.abort();
      }

      // Clear existing debounce
      clearTimeout(debounceTimer);

      // Set new debounce
      debounceTimer = setTimeout(async () => {
        // Check global concurrency limit
        if (globalInFlight >= maxConcurrent) {
          await waitForSlot();
        }

        globalInFlight++;
        abortController = new AbortController();

        try {
          const response = await fetch(`${fetchUrl}?q=${term}`, {
            signal: abortController.signal
          });

          if (!abortController.signal.aborted) {
            const suggestions = await response.json();
            updateFieldUI(suggestions);
          }
        } catch (error) {
          if (error.name !== 'AbortError') {
            console.error('Fetch error:', error);
          }
        } finally {
          globalInFlight--;
        }
      }, 300);
    }
  };
}
```

## Concept Overview
A custom useDebounce hook defers function execution until a specified delay period has elapsed, making it essential for optimizing search inputs, API calls, and other performance-critical operations in React applications.

## Implementation Details
- Utilize useRef to maintain the latest callback reference, preventing stale closures across renders
- Implement comprehensive cleanup logic in useEffect to cancel pending debounced invocations
- Return a useCallback wrapper with stable dependencies for optimal concurrent rendering performance
- Handle component unmounting scenarios to prevent memory leaks and unexpected behavior
- Ensure proper cleanup of timers to maintain application stability

## Code Example
```javascript
import { useCallback, useRef, useEffect } from 'react';

export function useDebounce(callback, delay) {
  const callbackRef = useRef(callback);
  const timeoutRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    callbackRef.current = callback;
  }, [callback]);

  useEffect(() => {
    return () => {
      if (timeoutRef.current) {
        clearTimeout(timeoutRef.current);
      }
    };
  }, []);

  return useCallback((...args) => {
    if (timeoutRef.current) {
      clearTimeout(timeoutRef.current);
    }

    timeoutRef.current = setTimeout(() => {
      callbackRef.current(...args);
    }, delay);
  }, [delay]);
}
```

## Key Benefits
- Prevents stale closures through proper ref management
- Ensures cleanup on component unmount
- Maintains stable reference for concurrent rendering
- Handles edge cases and prevents memory leaks

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests mastery of closures, promises, and per-key caching with real-world constraints like deduplicating in-flight requests, enforcing concurrency limits, and implementing TTL-based invalidation.

## Key Concepts

- **Closures and per-key cache state**: Using closures to maintain cache and in-flight maps private to each loader instance
- **In-flight deduplication**: Sharing promises for concurrent requests to the same key to prevent redundant fetches
- **Concurrency control**: Implementing a semaphore pattern with a 3-request cap
- **TTL-based caching**: Storing expiration timestamps and evicting stale entries
- **Background refresh**: Silently refetching expired keys without blocking callers
- **Invalidation semantics**: Manual cache invalidation for real-time scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
function createResourceLoader(fetcher) {
  const cache = new Map();
  const inFlight = new Map();
  let active = 0;
  const maxConcurrent = 3;
  const waiting = [];

  const acquire = () => new Promise(resolve => {
    if (active < maxConcurrent) {
      active++;
      resolve();
    } else {
      waiting.push(resolve);
    }
  });

  const release = () => {
    if (waiting.length > 0) {
      waiting.shift()();
    } else {
      active--;
    }
  };

  return {
    load(key, ttl = 5000) {
      const cached = cache.get(key);
      const now = Date.now();

      if (cached) {
        if (cached.expiry > now) {
          return cached.value;
        }
        if (!inFlight.has(key)) {
          fetcher(key).then(value => {
            cache.set(key, { value, expiry: Date.now() + ttl });
          });
        }
      }

      if (inFlight.has(key)) {
        return inFlight.get(key);
      }

      const promise = acquire().then(async () => {
        try {
          const value = await fetcher(key);
          cache.set(key, { value, expiry: Date.now() + ttl });
          return value;
        } finally {
          release();
          inFlight.delete(key);
        }
      });

      inFlight.set(key, promise);
      return promise;
    },

    invalidate(key) {
      cache.delete(key);
    }
  };
}

// Mock fetcher
const fetchUser = createResourceLoader(async (id) => {
  console.log(`Fetching user ${id}...`);
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 100));
  return { id, name: `User ${id}` };
});

// Usage
await fetchUser.load('user:1', 2000);
await fetchUser.load('user:1'); // Uses cache
fetchUser.invalidate('user:1');
await fetchUser.load('user:1'); // Refetches
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to implement a real-world cache with per-key TTL, deduplicated async work, and a lightweight pub/sub hook using closures.

## Key Concepts
- Per-key state with closures
- Promise deduplication across concurrent callers
- TTL-based invalidation and manual refresh
- Simple pub/sub for updates without external libs

## Code Example
```javascript
function createNotificationCache(fetchFn, ttlMs) {
  const store = new Map();
  return {
    get(userId) {
      const entry = store.get(userId) || { data: null, inFlight: null, expiry: 0, listeners: [] };
      if (entry.inFlight) return entry.inFlight;
      const p = fetchFn(userId).then(d => {
        entry.data = d;
        entry.expiry = Date.now() + ttlMs;
        entry.inFlight = null;
        return d;
      }).catch(e => { entry.inFlight = null; throw e; });
      entry.inFlight = p;
      store.set(userId, entry);
      return p;
    },
    refresh(userId) {
      const entry = store.get(userId) || { inFlight: null };
      const p = fetchFn(userId).then(d => {
        entry.data = d; entry.expiry = Date.now() + ttlMs; entry.inFlight = null; return d;
      });
      entry.inFlight = p; store.set(userId, entry); return p;
    },
    onUpdate(userId, cb) {
      const entry = store.get(userId) || { listeners: [] };
      entry.listeners.push(cb);
      store.set(userId, entry);
      return () => {
        entry.listeners = entry.listeners.filter(x => x !== cb);
      };
    }
  };
}
```

